{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1560600-inspirational-reading-for-aberrant","id":1560600,"title":"Inspirational reading for Aberrant","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: Aberrant"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-06-13T13:39:24-07:00","id":1560600,"title":"Inspirational reading for Aberrant","content":"If anybody has books that could be Aberrant setting adjacent or simply materials for one-shots, feel free to write about em.<br /> <br /> For me its the &quot;<b>Understand</b>&quot; a science fiction novelette by Ted Chiang, about a man developing super intelligence, similar to Flowers for Algernon.<br /> <br /> Without spoiling the content, there are ''lethal thoughts'', basilisk hacks and developing the future of humanity, similarly to Dune.<br /> <br /> The comic book <b>''The Power Fantasy'' </b>contains a great depiction of the megadeaths each main character could cause.<br /> <br /> Basically its about mutual destruction, deception on an individual and grand scale, the human factor in these inhumans. Its great.<br /> <br /> Anybody got any other ones, feel free to share<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29081-blink55","name":"Blink55","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2026"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-14T08:26:40-07:00","id":1560619,"title":"","content":"<b>The Boys</b> is an automatic choice here. The consequences of misusing superpowers are a major issue. Corporations trying to use people as objects is a real threat (it's a threat in our reality). Alienating people from being human is a serious theme as well. <b>Homelander </b>became a monster because no one ever allowed him a chance to be a person.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-15T17:51:23-07:00","id":1560645,"title":"","content":"The <b>Paragons</b> campaign setting for Mutants &amp; Masterminds 2nd edition, by Steve Kenson. My initial reaction to the book was &quot;this is what 1st edition Aberrant should have been.&quot; It's a toolkit for building the setting the way you want, with a collection of groups and individuals you can use, tweak, or ignore as you see fit. A lot of them can work in TC: Aberrant without much alteration (aside from the obvious bit about having to stat them for Storypath).<br /> <br /> <b>Ultra: Seven Days</b> by the Luna Brothers. Essentially a week in the life of a female superhero in a world where superheroes have to deal with celebrity gossip, awards shows, and the like in addition to regular superheroics. And refreshingly free of the cynicism-bordering-on-nihilism you see in a lot of these things. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-11T11:05:36-07:00","id":1561608,"title":"","content":"<b>Rising Stars</b> by J. Michael Straczynski is VERY similar to Aberrant in terms of tone and aesthetic. It has the same basic premise, with one major exception: all of the supers were born and raised in the same small town that was the only one affected, so while there are thousands of them, they all know each other at least a little bit in that small town kind of way. It's a very good source of inspiration for personality conflicts between Novas.<br /> <br /> I also think Kohei Horokoshi's <b>My Hero Academia</b> is a useful source of inspiration. I think that Aberrant is what you get when you try to synthesize the vibes of The Boys and MHA, so looking at both of them for the two extremes of what a &quot;superhuman society&quot; might look like could be helpful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2403-the-question","name":"The Question","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}